  "step_3": "The decision reason requires calculating an aspiration level as the mean of all outcomes (1225 Euros) and then comparing the probability of outcomes exceeding this level. The individual did not mention any calculation of a mean or a reference to an aspiration level. However, they did focus on the probability of a positive outcome (winning) and compared the probabilities (34% vs. 33%) — which aligns with the reason’s rule of preferring the lottery with higher probability of positive differences from the aspiration level. Although the individual did not explicitly use the aspiration level concept, their justification (favoring B due to higher probability of a win, despite small gain difference) matches the outcome of the reason’s rule. The key difference is that the individual did not compute or refer to the aspiration level, but their reasoning process leads to the same choice as the reason. Therefore, while the individual did not explicitly use the formal mechanism of the reason, the core logic (prioritizing higher probability of positive outcome) is consistent with the reason’s rule. The assessment is not certain, but strongly suggestive. Hence, the reason was likely used in an implicit or intuitive way.",
  "final_assessment_score": 75
}

